# What must not go into the image.
#
# Without this the build context is the whole working tree, and the developer's
# own 181 MB virtualenv, the caches and `stale/` all shipped to Cloud Run — the
# image was 456 MB of which most was never executed. Worse than the size: a
# production image that carries a second Python environment gives anyone who
# gets a shell in it a set of tools and a copy of code that is not the code
# being run.

# Local environments and caches. The image builds its own venv in the builder
# stage; this one is somebody's laptop.
**/.venv/
**/venv/
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/.pytest_cache/
**/.mypy_cache/
**/.ruff_cache/
**/.hypothesis/
**/.coverage
**/htmlcov/
**/*.egg-info/
**/dist/
**/build/

# Code that no longer runs. Kept in the repository, not maintained, and
# definitely not deployed.
backend/stale/

# Uploaded documents from a local run. These are patient scans in every
# environment that matters; baking them into an image would distribute them.
backend/uploads/

# Secrets and local config. `.env` is gitignored too — this is the second lock,
# because a build context is assembled from the filesystem, not from git.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
**/*.pem
**/*.key
**/service-account*.json

# Version control and tooling.
.git/
.github/
.gitignore
.claude/

# Documentation and infrastructure. Neither is read at runtime; the deploy
# scripts run from a checkout, not from inside the image.
docs/
infra/
# …except the two files the dashboard image copies in. `infra/` is excluded
# wholesale because the deploy scripts have no business being inside a running
# container; these are build inputs, which is a different thing.
!infra/docker/dashboard.nginx.conf
!infra/docker/dashboard.Dockerfile
*.md
Makefile
docker-compose.yml

# The dashboard's dependency tree. The image installs its own from the lockfile
# in its build stage; this one is somebody's laptop, and it is 300 MB.
dashboard/node_modules/
dashboard/dist/
dashboard/test-results/
dashboard/playwright-report/
